                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_40\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit describes two bugs in the 'words' FTS3 table ID-offsetting scheme:\n1. In MmsProvider.java, the MMS part row ID was computed as `(2 << 32) + rowId`, but\n   since Java's `<<` shift amount is taken modulo 32 for `int` operands, `2 << 32`\n   evaluates to `2 << 0` == 2, not the intended large offset. The fix changes this to\n   `(1L << 33) + rowId`, using a `long` literal to get the correct shift.\n2. In MmsSmsDatabaseHelper.java, `populateWordsTable` (used on DB upgrade) inserted MMS\n   part rows into the words table using the raw part `_id` with no offset at all\n   (`cv.put(Telephony.MmsSms.WordsTable.ID, id);`), colliding with SMS row IDs. The fix\n   adds the `(1L << 33) + id` offset for these rows.\n\ncandidate_40 is the commit that originally introduced this exact \"words\" table\nindexing scheme, and it contains both defects verbatim:\n- In MmsProvider.java: `cv.put(Telephony.MmsSms.WordsTable.ID, (2 << 32) + rowId);`\n  with the comment \"The row ids from the part table start at 2 << 32\" \u2014 the precise\n  buggy shift expression later corrected by the fix commit.\n- In MmsSmsDatabaseHelper.java's new `populateWordsTable` method, the MMS/part branch\n  inserts `cv.put(Telephony.MmsSms.WordsTable.ID, id);` with no offset applied at all,\n  identical to the second bug described in the fix commit message (\"The\n  populateWordsTable method... didn't add the offset at all\").\n\nNo other candidate (other than candidate_136, which is the fix commit itself, and it\nwas excluded since fixes cannot introduce the bug they fix) contains the `2 << 32`\nexpression or the unoffset populateWordsTable insert for MMS parts. candidate_40 is\ntherefore the first and only commit that introduces both problems, making it the\nbug-introducing commit.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
